WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.880
Today, we're back with unintended consequences, episode 33, for June 18, 2026.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:12.380
This is a show about what happens when smart, well-meaning people try to fix something,

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:19.100
and the world fixes back. In a small apartment in Orange, New Jersey, in 1922,

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:26.660
a 22-year-old dial painter named Grace Fryer noticed her teeth loosening and her jaw aching.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.060
She had spent four years at the United States Radium Corporation,

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:37.180
applying a luminous paint that contained radium. The paint made military watches and instruments

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:43.340
readable at night. Supervisors had shown the young women how to keep a fine point on each brush

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:49.300
by drawing the bristles between their lips. That technique delivered a small daily dose of the

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:55.580
radioactive material straight into their mouths. Over hundreds of shifts, the repeated motion meant

00:00:55.580 --> 00:01:01.080
the bristles passed her lips dozens of times each hour, and because the paint contained radium

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:06.580
salts mixed with zinc sulfide, the dose accumulated without any outward sign at first.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:14.160
Radium had been isolated by Marie and Pierre Curie in 1898. It entered commercial use because

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:20.920
it emitted a steady glow without external power. During World War I, military planners needed

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:26.500
reliable luminous markings on aircraft instruments, ship compasses, and trench watches.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:33.620
Troops had to operate in darkness. Factories therefore mixed radium salts was zinc sulfide to create

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:39.460
paint that would shine for years. The companies viewed the material as both a technical solution

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:46.160
and a public good. Early medical literature had even explored radium as a possible treatment for

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:51.860
certain tumors. That reinforced the sense that the element was beneficial when handled properly.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:59.580
One obvious objection at the time was whether any risk existed at all, because the only documented

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:05.980
problem involved skin burns from handling large research quantities. Smaller amounts in paint

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:12.840
seemed harmless by comparison, and the wartime need for readable dials in low light outweighed

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:19.840
any unmeasured internal hazard. By 1917, the United States radium corporation in orange

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:26.820
and the radium dial company in Ottawa, Illinois, were hiring dozens of young women, many still in their

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:33.320
teens, to paint dials at piecework rates. The work required steady hands and speed,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:40.420
foreman therefore taught the lip-pointing method as standard practice. Production records from the

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:46.500
orange plant show thousands of dials completed each week. The glowing watches reach both military

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:52.080
contracts and civilian markets. Company physicians occasionally examined the workers.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:58.720
Those examinations focused on general fitness rather than radiation exposure. At the time,

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:04.620
the only widely recognized hazard of radium was the skin burns suffered by researchers who handled

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:11.780
large quantities. If someone asked why the piece-rate system itself mattered, the arithmetic is straight

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:18.240
forward. Each additional dial meant a few more cents, so any technique that led a painter finish one

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:24.540
more instrument per hour was adopted immediately, and lip-pointing saved the seconds that would otherwise

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:31.340
be spent reshaping bristles by hand. The radium that lodged in bone and jaw tissue emitted alpha

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:39.440
particles continuously. It destroyed surrounding tissue and eventually caused necrosis. By 1924,

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:46.420
several orange workers had developed severe anemia and holes in their jaw bones. Dentist removed teeth

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:53.840
and sections of bone without stopping the decay. Grace Fryer and four colleagues filed suit in 1927

00:03:54.380 --> 00:04:00.140
after learning their conditions were linked to the paint. Internal company documents later showed

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:07.560
that management had received warnings from a Harvard physiologist in 1924. The company continued

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:13.960
the lip-pointing instruction anyway. The five plaintiffs received modest settlements in 1928.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:20.760
By then, at least 30 former dial painters had died. Many more carried lifelong injuries.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:27.380
The causal chain was straight forward, an incentive system rewarded speed. In complete knowledge

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:33.940
of alpha particle effects inside the body turned a precision technique into a slow cumulative poison.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:40.460
One might wonder whether simply washing hands would have changed the outcome. Because alpha particles

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:45.960
travel only short distances, external cleaning offered little protection once the material had

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:52.040
already been swallowed in tiny daily increments, and the particles remained embedded in bone,

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:58.400
where they continued emitting for years. The New Jersey cases prompted state labor departments to

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:05.700
ban lip-pointing. They required safer application methods instead. In 1934, the federal government

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:12.540
established the first occupational exposure limits for radium. Later lawsuits against radium firms

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:18.500
helped shape workers' compensation statutes. They also contributed to the creation of the Atomic

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:25.180
Energy Commission's safety standards after World War II. Today, the former orange plant site is a

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:31.460
superfund location. Surviving dial painter records are preserved by the National Museum of Nuclear

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:38.300
Science and History. No new commercial product has repeated the same combination of internal ingestion

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:44.340
and long-term alpha emission. The timeline itself shows how slowly the gap closed.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:51.180
Warnings arrived in 1924, yet the practice continued until lawsuits forced change,

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:55.620
and even then the first formal limits took another decade to appear.

00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:02.920
When a new material appears both useful and exotic, organizations tend to adopt it faster than they

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:09.660
can measure its full biological effects. Incentive structures that reward output while treating safety

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:14.860
as an afterthought will reliably produce workarounds that later prove costly.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:21.680
Modern regulators still confront the same pattern with engineered nanomaterials and certain medical

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:29.640
isotopes. Early enthusiasm outpaces longitudinal data in those fields. Consider the current push to

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:36.520
integrate new battery materials into consumer electronics and electric vehicles. What indicators would

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:42.560
you watch to see whether safety testing is keeping pace with production incentives? We'll leave it

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:47.840
there for today. Thanks for thinking through this one with me. If you know someone who loves a good

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:54.900
cautionary tale, send it their way, and I'll see you tomorrow with another. And before you go, this show

